protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"ridicule\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The maxim dismisses the cited research as a matter of feelings without establishing that diagnosis. B has offered an expertise-based reason for trusting consensus (E1935–1937), and subsequently invokes Wigner's retraction (E1964–1973). Those reasons qualify the surrounding argument but do not earn this personal dismissal; general insulation against criticism remains unclear.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2307,\n      \"endEvent\": 2311,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 2269,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 2340,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"most parsimonious most probable is always going to be the kind of naturalistic scientific explanation\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The unrestricted 'always' could exclude contrary evidence before examination; past explanatory success does not establish that universal conclusion. However, B supplies an inductive rationale (E2275–2289) and later makes rejection conditional on explaining the same evidence naturally (E2326–2329). These qualifications leave substitution and protective use genuinely uncertain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2698,\n      \"endEvent\": 2702,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 2688,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 2719,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"God is not an engineer he's the artist\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Asked why God would create complexity, A substitutes an artist's presumed enjoyment for evidence of God's purpose. Permission to create 'anything as complicated as he wants' (E2701–2702) makes complexity unable to challenge this account. A fairly qualifies the accompanying beauty judgment as subjective and not scientifically demonstrable (E2703–2709).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"contrasts\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 860,\n      \"endEvent\": 880,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"sure simple is great but if it doesn't explain all the data\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A qualifies simplicity by explanatory adequacy and supplies a cosmological example (E874–877). The compact conclusion summarizes an articulated comparison standard rather than replacing a reason.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 3333,\n      \"endEvent\": 3346,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"if someone else has a mind that I can expect them to act in a certain way\",\n      \"explanation\": \"B explains belief in other minds through expected behavior and testable predictions, while distinguishing justification from certainty (E3342–3346). This provides a reason and an explicit limit rather than merely asserting a settled conclusion.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}
